WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.977
This is a debugging exercise.

00:00:01.977 --> 00:00:07.842
Consider three cases. In the first case, the correct document appears at rank seven.

00:00:07.842 --> 00:00:11.117
That suggests a retrieval or ranking problem.

00:00:11.117 --> 00:00:17.592
In the second case, the correct chunk is rank one but the generated answer invents a detail.

00:00:17.592 --> 00:00:21.677
That points us toward the prompt or the language model behavior.

00:00:21.677 --> 00:00:25.872
In the third case, the system returns an old policy.

00:00:25.872 --> 00:00:29.058
That suggests an indexing or freshness problem.

00:00:29.678 --> 00:00:34.150
For each case, write down the first diagnostic step you would take.

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:37.905
There is intentionally no completed answer on the slide.

00:00:37.905 --> 00:00:42.650
The goal is to practice locating the failure before proposing a tool.

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:49.180
In real RAG work, this reasoning is more valuable than memorizing another library.

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:54.185
A good engineer asks, 'Which layer failed, and what evidence do I have?'
